🟢 Stage 1: Beginner (Foundations)

👉 Goal: Get comfortable with basic usage and CRUD.

What is MongoDB? (NoSQL vs SQL, when to use)

Document Model (BSON, JSON-like documents)

Databases & Collections

CRUD operations (Insert, Find, Update, Delete)

Basic queries ($eq, $gt, $in, $and/$or)

Projection (select fields)

Sorting, limit, skip

MongoDB Shell (mongosh) basics

📌 Output of this stage → You can use MongoDB like a normalDB for small apps.

🟡 Stage 2: Intermediate (Schema & Querying)

👉 Goal: Learn to model data properly and query efficiently.

Schema Design

Embedded vs Referenced

One-to-One, One-to-Many, Many-to-Many

Common Schema Patterns (Bucket, Outlier, Extended Reference, Tree)

Querying

Array queries ($elemMatch, $size, $all)

Text search ($text)

Aggregation Basics → $match, $group, $project, $sort, $limit, $lookup, $unwind

Indexes (single field, compound, multikey, text, TTL)

📌 Output of this stage → You can design schemas for real apps and run complex queries.

---



🟠 Stage 3: Advanced (Internals & Optimization)

👉 Goal: Understand how MongoDB works under the hood and optimize.

Internals 

WiredTiger storage engine

Journaling & durability

Working set & memory management

Locking & concurrency model

Optimization

Explain plans (.explain("executionStats"))

Indexing strategies (covering indexes, index intersection)

Aggregation optimization (push $match early)

Avoiding $skip for pagination → use range queries or cursor-based

Bulk operations (bulkWrite)

Transactions & Consistency

Multi-document transactions

Write Concern & Read Concern

Retryable writes

📌 Output of this stage → You can debug slow queries, choose right indexes, and handle transactions safely.

🔴 Stage 4: Production / Expert

👉 Goal: Be enterprise-ready for scale, security, and reliability.

Replication & High Availability

Replica sets & elections

Oplog internals

Failover testing

Sharding (Horizontal Scaling)

Config servers & mongos

Choosing shard keys (hotspot avoidance)

Chunk balancing

Security

Authentication (SCRAM, x.509)

Role-based access control (RBAC)

Encryption (at rest, in transit, CSFLE)

Connecting with Backend

Drivers (Node.js, Python, Java, etc.)

Connection pooling (poolSize, maxIdleTimeMS, keepAlive)

ODMs (Mongoose, Prisma MongoDB connector)

Advanced Features

Change Streams (real-time events)

Triggers (Atlas/Realm)

Time series collections

Geospatial queries

GridFS for large files

Ops & Best Practices

Backups (mongodump, mongorestore, Atlas Backup)

Monitoring (Atlas metrics, mongostat, mongotop)

Disaster Recovery Planning

Schema versioning strategy

Multi-tenancy (shared vs isolated DBs)

📌 Output of this stage → You can run MongoDB in production at scale, secure it, and keep it healthy.